nd1670-1732  aPERSO_NAME00225nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000260007494200150010039584OI20250128110315.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aPaassen, Catalien van  aPERSO_NAME00218nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000190007494200150009339586OI20250128112605.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aCott, Jonathan  aPERSO_NAME00218nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000190007494200150009339587OI20250128112831.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aMeier, Gabriel  aPERSO_NAME01417nz  a2200253n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001560007436600270023050000330025750000450029051000870033552003090042255000710073155000610080255000690086355000590093255000860099166700160107790500550109394200150114839588OI20260130160841.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aThe Art of Fingering on the Pianoforte: A Comprehensive and Comparative Study of Clementi’s Didactical Works Applied to Period and Modern Instruments  k2022/09/01l2032/06/30  aBeghin, Tom4Promoter917739  aZafeiropoulos, Anastasios4Fellow926639  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThis research project proposes a complete methodology concerning the treatment of 18th- and 19th-century keyboard methods, aiming to enrich our understanding and perspectives of historically informed performance based on an analyses of Clementi’s didactical opuses from the point of view of fingerings.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aFingering1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q608487921861  aKeyboard music1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q105715630921412  aMethod1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2250960921402  aHistorically informed performance1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1344860920980  aNot present  a24114Principal fundingk2022/09/01l2032/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME01932nz  a2200217n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711000680007436600270014250000380016950000330020751000870024052011680032755000710149555000620156666700160162890500550164494200150169939589OI20260130161350.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aTopologies: From Field Recording to Phonography and The Virtual  k2022/09/01l2032/06/30  aImpett, Jonathan4Promoter916357  aDantas, Paulo4Fellow926637  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThe insights leading to the present project firstly manifested themselves in 2008, when I worked with field recordings on the basis of relatively well-established notions in music composition studies, such as ‘musical material’ and écriture. With hindsight, he understands the outcomes of those first experiments as indication of sound’s ‘irreducibility’ (echoing artist and theorist Rodolfo Caesar, who suggests that “the task of accounting for [sounds’] meanings, complexities, scope and connotations through translations, approximations, schematics, analogies or other 'reductions' is endless”). Embracing this irreducibility, he will explore field recording as an activity that has the potential to create “transaction spaces”, facilitating exchanges and negotiations both between diverse practices that use sound as a relevant component (such as ‘music composition’ and ‘fieldwork registering’) and notions usually presented as dichotomic (‘abstract sounding’, ‘documental rhetorics’, etc.). How can those transaction spaces be explored? And how far can we stretch them, without jeopardizing field recording’s identity?  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aSound art1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1744327926713  aNot present  a24114Principal fundingk2022/09/01l2032/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME02774nz  a2200217n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001100007436600270018450000370021150000350024851000870028352019560037055000710232655000730239766700160247090500550248694200150254139590OI20260130161304.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aNew Methods for Music Composition with AI: Music Synthesis and Analysis with Periodic Spacetime Sequences  k2021/09/01l2031/06/30  aAssis, Paulo de4Promoter916044  aŁukawski, Adam9266424Fellow  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThe research project investigates the role of Artificial Intelligence in the artistic practice of a human composer in which AI is used to compose new music. The outcomes of this research project are not only new music compositions, but also a set of new technologically-supported methods for music composition. For the project, a new software is developed: AI music composer’s assistant supporting human composer with the power of AI.  The research is especially focused on creating a general-purpose musical Environment which was initially inspired by notions coming from:  Assemblage Theory and its notion of topological diagram (Gilles Deleuze, Felix Guattari, Manuel Delanda, and Paulo de Assis), the periodicity of the Shepard tone illusion, the concept of a strange loop by cognitive scientist Douglas Hofstadter. The project proposes a post-human definition of a musical work as resulting from the feedback loop created by Observers (humans or AI agents) interacting with Environments. Environment is an n-dimensional space with a set of rules for changing it, for instance: a music score with rules for modifying pitch, duration and dynamics, or a virtual 3D space with physical rules for interacting with objects in virtual realities, such as the metaverse, and computer games). In my definition, inspired by the Reinforcement Learning branch of machine learning, interactions of Observers with Environments are named Performative Transactions. These interactions are recorded in blocks, and form blockchains (that become “compositions”). Blockchains are shareable between Observers enabling exploration, collaboration, and modification of the created sequences of events. During the course of the project different Environments are built. Observers (human researcher and developed AI agents) interact with them to create new music compositions.  LOGO Orpheus Kleingebruik Universiteit Leiden logo Kon Con Image 3 Image 6 KU Leuven logo  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aArtificial intelligence1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1166092413  aNot present  a24114Principal fundingk2021/09/01l2031/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME01276nz  a2200217n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711000850007436600270015950000380018650000340022451000870025852004450034555000710079055000610086155000660092290500550098894200150104339591OI20260130160523.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aMediation and Object-Specific Creative Processes in Experimental Sound Practices  k2020/09/01l2030/06/30  aImpett, Jonathan4Promoter916357  aCaliman, Magno4Fellow925652  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aThis research investigates the dynamic of negotiation artists undergo when embracing programming languages and DIY electronics as objects of inquiry, and how the opaquenes of those technologies informs both processes and results of art-making. An "object-specific" creative/research practice where inhabiting the border of a reconfigurable apparatus becomes a method for discovering potentialities and limits in both the device and himself.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aTechnology1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q11016921633  aElectronic music1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q9778922014  a24114Principal fundingk2020/09/01l2030/06/30f1  aCORPO_NAME00218nz  a2200109n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006710000190007494200150009339592OI20250128121122.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aMeersmans, Jos  aPERSO_NAME01909nz  a2200229n  450000100060000000300030000600500170000900800410002604000070006711001490007436600270022350000330025050000380028351000870032152009950040855000710140355000570147455000620153166700160159390500550160994200150166439593OI20260130160943.0250128|| aca||aabn           | a|a     d  aOI  aRewriting for the Salon: La Pratique de l’arrangement pour piano accompagné dans les salons parisiens de la première moitié du XIXe siècle  k2018/09/01l2028/06/30  aBeghin, Tom4Promoter917739  aMontebugnoli, Luca4Fellow925649  aOrpheus Instituut (Ghent, Belgium)1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q2289519922507  aAu début du XIXe siècle l’art de l’arrangement vit son âge d’or : à une époque qui ne connait pas encore de reproduction technique du son, la publication de transcriptions de symphonies, concertos et opéra pour petits ensembles permet l’écoute ou l’évocation au salon des œuvres destinées au concert public ou au théâtre. La redécouverte de cette pratique dans toute son ampleur et sa créativité est le propos de ce projet de recherche. A travers l’analyse et l’étude musicale des partitions, il ambitionne non seulement à la compréhension historique et esthétique du phénomène, mais à la reconstruction et l’expérimentation d’un savoir-faire, celui de l’arrangement, qui était propre aux musiciens de l’époque et que la modernité a progressivement oublié. La vie musicale parisienne dans la première moitié du XIXe siècle fera de cadre historique à la recherche en raison de la centralité que la ville acquiert au cours de la période.  aArtistic research1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q20827087921804  aSalon1http://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q576342923251  aArrangement1http://www.wikidata.org/ent